## The `HolRefute` Library

\label{sec:HolRefute}
\index{HolRefute|(}
\index{counterexamples|see {HolRefute}}
\index{model finding|see {HolRefute}}

`HolRefute` searches for finite counterexamples to HOL conjectures.  It
combines Quickcheck-style execution, native narrowing, and a
Kodkod-backed finite model finder behind the `Refute` structure.  It is
intended for finding mistakes quickly; a report that no counterexample
was found within the selected bounds is not a proof.

The library is available on Poly/ML builds.  The following tested
sequence starts an interactive session from the HOL source tree:

    cd src/HolRefute
    ../../bin/hol

At the prompt, load the public facade explicitly:

    load "Refute"; open Refute;

This loads the implementation modules and registers the built-in backends.
Loading and running Refute leaves no Refute-created type, constant, theorem,
or theory binding in the user's theory.  In particular, temporary
compilation artefacts are removed on success, failure, timeout, and
interruption.

### A first counterexample

The following is a static transcript from a Refute-enabled session.
Timing fields are machine-dependent, and reports are re-wrapped for the
page: an interactive session prints each one as a single
`<<HOL message: ...>>` block.  The configuration selects the portable
`Compute` execution substrate to make the example independent of an
external compiler.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- load "Refute"; open Refute;
(* load output omitted *)
> val it = () : unit

- the_config :=
    (default_config
       |> upd_sequential true
       |> upd_substrate Compute
       |> upd_size 4);
> val it = () : unit

- val _ = quickcheck ``(n : num) < 3``;
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s):
    n = 3
  Certified: |- ~!n. n < 3
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - load "Refute"; open Refute;
    (* load output omitted *)
    > val it = () : unit

    - the_config :=
        (default_config
           |> upd_sequential true
           |> upd_substrate Compute
           |> upd_size 4);
    > val it = () : unit

    - val _ = quickcheck ``(n : num) < 3``;
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s):
        n = 3
      Certified: ⊢ ¬∀n. n < 3
```

Free variables are implicitly universally closed.  The `bindings` field
of the returned structured counterexample contains `n = 3`, and its
`cert` field contains the displayed HOL theorem.  Refute reports through
the `"Refute"` trace at level 1.  Higher trace levels show backend and
substrate selection, schedules, and compiled plans; level 0 suppresses
normal Refute output.  `upd_quiet true` is the preferred per-call way to
suppress all messages and warnings.

### Entry points and results

The principal result type is:

```sml
datatype outcome =
    Counterexample of counterexample list
  | NoCounterexample
  | Model of counterexample list
  | NoModel
  | Unknown of string list
```

A `counterexample` records its `backend`, execution `substrate`,
`certainty`, free-variable `bindings`, requested `evals`, optional
certificate, optional finite `scope` and model, and statistics.
`NoCounterexample` means that a selected search covered the whole relevant
space: no counterexample is possible at any larger size.  The result is
decisive and stops the concurrent backend pool.  A clean fixed-bound search
that does not cover every relevant type is `Unknown`, with the tested scope
frontier in its reasons.  `Unknown` also carries timeout, inapplicability,
missing-generator, and other incomplete-search reasons.
Kodkod with `upd_falsify false` returns `Model` or `NoModel` instead;
backend selection never changes the meaning of `Counterexample` or
`NoCounterexample`.  Model search is auxiliary in a mixed backend run, so a
decisive counterexample result can preempt it.

The public entry points are:

| Entry point | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `refute cfg tm` | Search for a counterexample to `tm` with `cfg`, print the result unless quiet, and return the structured `outcome`. |
| `refute_with updates tm` | Apply `updates` to `!the_config`, then search `tm`. |
| `refute_goal cfg (asms, tm)` | Search the goal whose assumptions are `asms` and conclusion is `tm`. |
| `refute_goal_with updates goal` | The goal form of `refute_with`. |
| `REFUTE_TAC` | Inspect a tactic goal using `!the_config`, print the result, and leave the goal unchanged. |
| `QUICKCHECK_TAC` | As `REFUTE_TAC`, selecting exhaustive, random, and narrowing search. |
| `NARROWING_TAC` | As `REFUTE_TAC`, selecting only symbolic narrowing. |
| `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC` | As `REFUTE_TAC`, selecting only the relational model finder. |
| `REFUTE_TAC_WITH updates` | Apply per-call configuration updates and inspect the goal. |
| `REFUTE_CONFIG_TAC cfg` | Inspect the goal with exactly `cfg`. |
| `quickcheck tm` | Use `!the_config`, but select only the Quickcheck backends `exhaustive`, `random`, and `narrowing`. |
| `model_refute tm` | Use `!the_config`, but select only the `kodkod` model-finder backend. |
| `try_refute cfg goal` | Run quietly and return `SOME (backend, outcome)` only for a counterexample, or `NONE` for a miss, timeout, or inconclusive search. |
| `find_unused_assms cfgopt thy` | Probe the named theory and return possible unused-assumption sets. |

There are also conveniences `refute_def tm`, which calls `refute
!the_config tm`, and `refute_top ()`, which applies `refute_goal` to the
current top goal.

### Tactic interface and per-call selection

Most users should start with the parameterless diagnostic tactic:

```sml
REFUTE_TAC
```

It tries every backend selected by the interactive configuration.  Use
`QUICKCHECK_TAC`, `NARROWING_TAC`, or `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC` only when the
search method or counterexample representation matters.  All four tactics
print an outcome and return the original goal unchanged; they do not prove
or admit it.

Advanced calls can supply ordinary configuration updaters locally:

```sml
REFUTE_TAC_WITH
  [upd_search (Only [Narrowing]),
   upd_size 7,
   upd_timeout 5.0]
```

The updates have type `config -> config` and are applied to `!the_config`
from left to right.  Thus the last update of a field wins.  The global
configuration is read when the tactic is applied, not when it is
constructed.  The tactic honours every resulting field, including `quiet`
and `expect`.

For reproducible tools and regression tests, use an exact configuration:

```sml
REFUTE_CONFIG_TAC
  (default_config
     |> upd_search (Only [Narrowing])
     |> upd_size 7)
```

The built-in backend names are represented by a typed interface:

```sml
datatype backend_choice =
    Exhaustive
  | Random
  | Narrowing
  | ModelFinder
  | RegisteredBackend of string

datatype search =
    AllBackends
  | QuickcheckBackends
  | Only of backend_choice list
```

`RegisteredBackend name` selects an extension backend by its registered
name.  `Only []` is rejected.  `upd_search` converts a `search` into a
configuration updater.  `apply_updates updates cfg` constructs an exact
configuration, while `current_config updates` applies the same list to the
interactive configuration.  `refute_with` and `refute_goal_with` provide
the same local-update convention for callers that consume structured
outcomes.

`try_refute` is intended for other automation.  It preserves the
caller's backend selection but forces sequential execution, seed 42,
`NoExpectation`, and quiet mode.  Its result is structured; callers need
not parse Refute's printed text.  For example, this call
prints no Refute report:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- Option.map #1
    (try_refute
       (default_config
          |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
          |> upd_substrate Compute
          |> upd_size 4)
       ([], ``(n : num) < 3``));
> val it = SOME "exhaustive" : string option
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - Option.map #1
        (try_refute
           (default_config
              |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
              |> upd_substrate Compute
              |> upd_size 4)
           ([], ``(n : num) < 3``));
    > val it = SOME "exhaustive" : string option
```

### Backends, substrates, and racing

Four backends are registered by default, in increasing weight order:

| Weight | Backend | Search |
|---:|---|---|
| 20 | `exhaustive` | Size-bounded enumeration and evaluation. |
| 30 | `random` | Reproducible random generation and evaluation. |
| 40 | `narrowing` | Native symbolic narrowing, including prenex existentials. |
| 50 | `kodkod` | Relational finite-model search through Kodkodi. |

`upd_search AllBackends` selects every configured backend;
`QuickcheckBackends` selects exhaustive, random, and narrowing search; and
`Only choices` selects exactly its nonempty list.  Kodkod is absent from an
all-backend run when its external component is not available.  An
extension registered under a string name is selected through
`RegisteredBackend`.  Weights order sequential execution and break ties
between results of equal certainty; in parallel mode they do not promise
which process finishes first.

The default `sequential = false` races the selected backends.  A result
stops the race only when its certainty reaches the best ceiling declared
by every eligible backend.  Thus a quick potential counterexample does
not hide a genuine result that another selected backend can still
produce.  A backend registered through `register_backend` conservatively
declares the ceiling `Genuine`; a backend with a tighter ceiling can use
`register_backend_with_ceiling`.

One call owns one absolute search deadline of `timeout` seconds.
Preprocessing, backend admission, compilation, testing, and solver calls
all consume the same remainder; mandatory cleanup may finish slightly
after it.

Exhaustive and random testing compile a common test-plan language onto
one of three substrates:

* `NativeSML` extracts and compiles the executable fragment in process.
  It is the preferred fast substrate on Poly/ML.
* `Cv` compiles a fuel-bounded HOL test loop and evaluates its
  `cv_compute` translation.  It accepts a smaller, first-order fragment.
* `Compute` interprets plans with `computeLib`.  It is the compatibility
  substrate and supports registered custom and abstract generators.

The default `Auto` tries these in priority order: Native SML, Cv, then
Compute.  It falls through only when a substrate says it is inapplicable.
Selecting `NativeSML`, `Cv`, or `Compute` explicitly never silently
changes substrate; inapplicability then contributes an `Unknown` reason.
For a fixed `seed`, all three consume the same specified 64-bit random
stream.

Narrowing is a native backend rather than another choice in this
substrate ladder.  It searches refinement depths upward from 0: a fixed
`size` is its final depth, while the default iterative mode keeps
deepening past the initial window until the deadline.
Purely universal problems use plain narrowing; a prenex problem with an
existential uses the PNF engine when `allow_existentials` is true.
Function inputs are represented by finite update chains when
`finite_functions` is true, with constant functions used for
higher-order domains.  Partially known values are printed with `_` holes.

The model finder translates the original polymorphic problem into a
bounded relational formula.  It handles finite scopes, datatypes and
codatatypes, functions, sets, inductive and coinductive predicates,
typedefs, quotients, binary integers, machine words, and the registered
`rat` (default) and opt-in `real` fraction encodings.  Its `Scope:` line
records the exact finite interpretation.
For example:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- val cfg =
    default_config
      |> upd_search (Only [ModelFinder])
      |> upd_sequential true
      |> upd_card [(NONE, [2])];
(* configuration value omitted *)

- val _ = refute cfg ``(x : 'a) = y``;
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: kodkod, substrate: kodkod, 0.177s):
  Scope: card alpha = 2
    x = a2
    y = a1
  Certified: |- ~!x y. x = y
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - val cfg =
        default_config
          |> upd_search (Only [ModelFinder])
          |> upd_sequential true
          |> upd_card [(NONE, [2])];
    (* configuration value omitted *)

    - val _ = refute cfg ``(x : 'a) = y``;
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: kodkod, substrate: kodkod, 0.177s):
      Scope: card α = 2
        x = a2
        y = a1
      Certified: ⊢ ¬∀x y. x = y
```

A word type of concrete width `w` is a native carrier of exactly `2^w`
atoms rather than a datatype or a typedef, and models print surface word
literals.  Word literals, `n2w`, `w2n`, the arithmetic and bitwise
operations, the shifts, and the unsigned and signed orders are encoded
directly.  Width-changing and bit-permuting operations, such as `w2w`,
`word_concat`, the rotations, and `word_reverse`, are refused by name
instead of approximated, so an inconclusive report never passes for a
proof.  Word arithmetic uses Kodkod's integers, so a goal mentioning a
word disables binary integers rather than losing the bounds it depends
on.  A width left as a type variable has no carrier at all; such goals
are reached through the monomorphic width instances that `upd_widths`
selects, which the executable backends test as well.

### Smart testing

Refute rewrites executable bounded quantifiers before planning.  It
supports the universal forms `!n. n < e ==> P n`, `!n. n <= e ==> P n`,
`!n. n IN count e ==> P n`, and `!x. MEM x xs ==> P x`, and their
existential conjunction duals.  The rewrites use `EVERY` or `EXISTS`, so a
bound such as `LENGTH xs` may depend on another generated value.  Offset
intervals such as `lo <= n /\ n < hi` are not recognized; neither they nor
unrestricted quantifiers over infinite types become executable QC goals.

With `smart_quantifier = true`, premises are considered while the test
plan is built rather than after every variable has been generated.
Equality premises can bind variables directly.  With
`smart_generators = true`, a positive, first-order Horn premise recovered
from `Hol_reln`, or from Horn-shaped Boolean function equations, can be
mode-compiled to an `Enum` plan.  `depth` is the fixed fuel supplied to
such an enumerator.  Premises may be reordered during mode analysis;
`reorder_premises = false` disables that step.  If mode compilation is not
possible, Refute safely falls back to generate-and-guard testing.

The practical difference is visible at small bounds.  At `size = 1` and
`depth = 8`, the Horn-function premise in the following example directly
generates a distinct list and finds a certified counterexample.  With
smart generators disabled, generate-and-test does not reach one at the
same bound and reports an incomplete no-hit result.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- val cfg =
    default_config
      |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
      |> upd_substrate Compute
      |> upd_sequential true
      |> upd_size 1
      |> upd_depth 8;
(* configuration value omitted *)

- val _ = refute cfg
    ``ALL_DISTINCT (xs : bool list) ==> LENGTH xs < 1``;
(* temporary compilation messages omitted *)
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 1, 0.075s):
    xs = [T]
  Certified: |- ~!xs. ALL_DISTINCT xs ==> LENGTH xs < 1
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - val cfg =
        default_config
          |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
          |> upd_substrate Compute
          |> upd_sequential true
          |> upd_size 1
          |> upd_depth 8;
    (* configuration value omitted *)

    - val _ = refute cfg
        ``ALL_DISTINCT (xs : bool list) ==> LENGTH xs < 1``;
    (* temporary compilation messages omitted *)
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 1, 0.075s):
        xs = [T]
      Certified: ⊢ ¬∀xs. ALL_DISTINCT xs ⇒ LENGTH xs < 1
```

Smart-enumerator fuel bounds every generated derivation.  Exhausting an
`Enum` or smart guard is consequently incomplete even if its finite
search happened not to find a hit.

### Certification and certainty

Execution substrates, generated SML, Kodkodi, and SAT solvers are
untrusted accelerators.  With the default `certify = true`, a Quickcheck
candidate is re-evaluated independently with `computeLib`.  A replay that
proves the instantiated proposition true discards the candidate; one that
proves it false attaches a theorem for the negated, closed instance.  If
direct evaluation is stuck, ordinary Quickcheck certification may normalize
the closure to PNF and replay an all-universal prefix using the candidate's
exact generated bindings.  This fallback remains partial for mixed or
existential PNF prefixes.  An inconclusive attempt keeps the
substrate-derived certainty with `cert = NONE`.  Certification is monotone:
it only discards false positives or adds theorems, and never weakens a
verdict.  Narrowing
grounds partial hits before evaluation and certifies PNF existential hits
by replaying their finite case tree.  Refute never adds an oracle tag.

The fields `certainty` and `cert` deliberately answer different
questions, and neither one determines the other.  `certainty` is the
semantic strength of the result: `Genuine` says that the backend's
semantic conditions for a real counterexample hold.  `cert = SOME thm`
says only that Refute additionally constructed a HOL theorem.  For the
model finder the certainty is decided by the encoding, not by the
certificate: Refute trusts Kodkodi exactly as Isabelle/HOL's Nitpick
trusts Kodkod, so a translation that is sound and exact yields
`Genuine`, one resting on an approximation yields
`QuasiGenuine reasons`, and an unsound one yields `Potential reasons`.
The model finder additionally attempts a fail-closed kernel replay of
reconstructed values; when that replay cannot close the negated goal,
the result remains `Genuine` with `cert = NONE`, exactly as for an
uncertified QC hit below.
The backend and substrate fields make clear whether a report came from
MF, narrowing, or executable QC.

Setting `certify = false` skips all QC EVAL and replay certification.  A
QC hit remains `Genuine`, but has `cert = NONE` and gets an explicit
uncertified marker:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- the_config := (!the_config |> upd_certify false);
> val it = () : unit

- val _ = quickcheck ``(n : num) < 3``;
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s):
    n = 3
  Certification: uncertified
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - the_config := (!the_config |> upd_certify false);
    > val it = () : unit

    - val _ = quickcheck ``(n : num) < 3``;
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s):
        n = 3
      Certification: uncertified
```

This opt-out is useful while exploring, but clients that require a kernel
object must inspect `cert`, not merely test `certainty = Genuine`.

### Finding unused assumptions

The unused-assumption interface has three levels:

```sml
check_unused_assms :
  config option -> string * thm -> string * int list list option
find_unused_assms :
  config option -> string -> (string * int list list option) list
print_unused_assms : config option -> string option -> unit
```

`NONE` derives a QC-only configuration from `!the_config`; `SOME cfg`
preserves the supplied backend selection.  Each probe nevertheless forces
sequential, quiet, abort-potential, expectation-free operation.  Probes
are serial.  An inconclusive `Unknown` is conservatively treated as "not
shown unused" rather than as evidence that an assumption can be removed.

For each theorem, the tool strips outer universal quantifiers and indexes
the left-to-right chain of implication premises exactly as written.  It
does not split a conjunctive antecedent: in `(p /\ q) ==> r`, `p /\ q` is
one premise at index 0.  The tool first tests every single indexed premise,
then grows combinations only from individually droppable premises and
returns the maximal simultaneously droppable sets.  Result indexes are
zero-based; `print_unused_assms` displays them one-based.  `NONE` in the
per-theorem result means that the theorem has no implication premises,
whereas `SOME []` means that no premise was shown droppable.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- val th = prove (``!p q. p ==> q ==> q``, simp []);
> val th = |- !p q. p ==> q ==> q : thm

- Refute.check_unused_assms (SOME cfg) ("demo", th);
> val it = ("demo", SOME [[0]])
    : string * int list list option
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - val th = prove (``!p q. p ==> q ==> q``, simp []);
    > val th = ⊢ ∀p q. p ⇒ q ⇒ q : thm

    - Refute.check_unused_assms (SOME cfg) ("demo", th);
    > val it = ("demo", SOME [[0]])
        : string * int list list option
```

`find_unused_assms cfgopt "foo"` checks all theorems returned by
`DB.thms "foo"`, sorted by name.  `print_unused_assms cfgopt NONE` checks
the current theory and prints a summary, including the number of
inconclusive probes.

### Configuration

Configurations are immutable records changed by updater functions.  The
usual idiom is:

```sml
val cfg = Refute.default_config
  |> Refute.upd_timeout 5.0
  |> Refute.upd_search
       (Refute.Only [Refute.Exhaustive, Refute.Narrowing])
  |> Refute.upd_size 7
```

`default_config` contains `default_qc_config` and `default_mf_config`.
`upd_qc` and `upd_mf` replace a complete sub-record; the individual
updaters are generally less brittle.  `the_config : config ref` supplies
the interactive defaults, and `show_config ()` prints its complete
current value.  Prefer `REFUTE_TAC_WITH` for a one-off tactic call; mutate
`the_config` only when changing persistent interactive defaults.  The
common fields are:

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_timeout` | `10.0` | Seconds in the whole-call search deadline. |
| `upd_search` | `AllBackends` | Typed selection of all, Quickcheck, or explicit backends. |
| `upd_sequential` | `false` | Run backends by weight instead of racing them. |
| `upd_genuine_only` | `false` | Suppress potential results and continue looking for genuine ones. |
| `upd_abort_potential` | `false` | Stop at the first counterexample regardless of certainty. |
| `upd_quiet` | `false` | Suppress Refute traces, messages, and warnings for this call. |
| `upd_no_assms` | `false` | Ignore goal assumptions supplied to `refute_goal`. |
| `upd_evals` | `[]` | Extra terms whose values should be reconstructed and displayed. |
| `upd_expect` | `NoExpectation` | Regression expectation checked after the run. |
| `upd_max_counterexamples` | `1` | Maximum QC counterexamples requested. |
| `upd_tag` | `""` | Text appended to the formatted outcome. |
| `upd_widths` | `[1,2,3,4]` | Widths tried for a word type variable; entries lie between 1 and 32. |

Expectations are `ExpectNone`, `ExpectUnknown`, `ExpectCex`, `ExpectModel`,
`ExpectNoModel`,
`ExpectGenuine`, `ExpectQuasiGenuine`, and `ExpectPotential`.  A mismatch
raises a HOL error.  They are intended mainly for tests.

#### Quickcheck and narrowing fields

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_size` | `10` | Maximum generated size and narrowing refinement depth; pins a fixed bound. |
| `upd_iterative_size` | default mode | Initial window for adaptive iterative deepening. |
| `upd_iterations` | `100` | Random draws. |
| `upd_depth` | `10` | Fixed smart-enumerator fuel for each plan. |
| `upd_finite_types` | `true` | Add small fresh finite types when monomorphizing type variables. |
| `upd_finite_type_size` | `3` | Number of such finite cardinalities considered. |
| `upd_default_type` | `[``:num``]` | Type instances used when `finite_types` is false. |
| `upd_substrate` | `Auto` | `Auto`, `NativeSML`, `Cv`, or `Compute` for executable QC. |
| `upd_allow_existentials` | `true` | Permit PNF narrowing of prenex existentials. |
| `upd_finite_functions` | `true` | Finitize narrowing function inputs as update chains or constants. |
| `upd_certify` | `true` | Independently certify QC and narrowing hits. |
| `upd_smart_quantifier` | `true` | Compile premises into binds, guards, and generators. |
| `upd_smart_generators` | `true` | Enable Horn relation/function `Enum` plans. |
| `upd_optimise_equality` | `true` | Turn suitable equality premises into direct bindings. |
| `upd_reorder_premises` | `true` | Reorder premises during smart mode inference. |
| `upd_seed` | `NONE` | Reproducible random seed; `NONE` advances a deterministic session stream. |

The default size mode is iterative deepening: `10` is an initial window,
not a terminal bound, and testing deepens until the deadline.  `upd_size`
deliberately pins a fixed, reproducible bound, while `upd_iterative_size`
sets a different initial window and restores adaptive deepening.  The
separate `depth` field bounds narrowing; it is not a second
0-through-depth schedule loop.

#### Model-finder fields

Several MF options are rows.  A `NONE` key is the fallback;
`SOME ty` or `SOME tm` overrides matching types or constants.  A Boolean
row value of `NONE` asks Refute to choose, while `SOME true` or
`SOME false` forces the choice.

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_card` | fallback `[1,...,10]` | Candidate finite cardinalities by type; pins fixed rows. |
| `upd_iterative_card` | default mode | Initial adaptive cardinality windows. |
| `upd_max` | fallback `[~1]` | Maximum constructor multiplicities; `~1` means automatic. |
| `upd_mono` | fallback `NONE` | Automatic or forced monotonicity classification by type. |
| `upd_wf` | fallback `NONE` | Automatic or forced well-founded treatment by constant. |
| `upd_sat_solver` | `"smart"` | Kodkodi SAT solver selection. |
| `upd_batch_size` | `50` | Scope problems submitted per solver batch. |
| `upd_falsify` | `true` | Falsify the conjecture; false searches for a model of it. |
| `upd_user_axioms` | `NONE` | Automatic or forced inclusion of user axioms. |
| `upd_destroy_constrs` | `true` | Simplify constructor applications during preprocessing. |
| `upd_total_consts` | `NONE` | Automatic or forced total relational encoding of constants. |
| `upd_peephole_optim` | `true` | Enable relational peephole optimization. |
| `upd_datatype_sym_break` | `5` | Datatype symmetry-breaking strength. |
| `upd_kodkod_sym_break` | `15` | Kodkod's symmetry-breaking limit. |
| `upd_max_potential` | `1` | Global budget for potential models. |
| `upd_max_genuine` | `1` | Global budget for genuine models. |
| `upd_atoms` | fallback `[]` | Preferred printed atom names by type. |
| `upd_format` | fallback `[1]` | Group curried model-function arguments into tuples. |
| `upd_show_types` | `false` | Include interpreted type universes in models. |
| `upd_show_skolems` | `true` | Include Skolem values. |
| `upd_show_consts` | `false` | Include reconstructed constant tables. |
| `upd_debug` | `false` | Enable MF debug mode and single-problem batches. |
| `upd_overlord` | `false` | Keep solver files with stable names in the current directory. |
| `upd_max_threads` | `0` | Kodkodi thread cap; zero leaves it unspecified. |
| `upd_tac_timeout` | `0.5` | Seconds for inner monotonicity and well-foundedness tactics. |
| `upd_specialize` | `true` | Specialize static higher-order arguments. |
| `upd_box` | fallback `NONE` | Automatically or forcibly box function and pair types. |
| `upd_binary_ints` | `NONE` | Automatically or forcibly use binary integers. |
| `upd_bits` | `[1,...,10]` | Candidate binary word widths. |
| `upd_star_linear_preds` | `true` | Use exact closure for linear inductive predicates. |
| `upd_iter` | fallback depth row | Fixpoint-unrolling depths for inductive predicates. |
| `upd_bisim_depth` | `[9]` | Codatatype bisimulation depths; `~1` disables bisimulation. |
| `upd_finitize` | fallback `NONE` | Automatically or forcibly finitize shallow infinite types. |
| `upd_whack` | `[]` | Constants whose definitions preprocessing should ignore. |
| `upd_need` | `NONE` | Optional terms whose values must occur in a model. |
| `upd_merge_type_vars` | `false` | Merge all MF type-variable scopes into the first variable. |

Card rows are iterative by default: the fallback `[1,...,10]` is an
initial window that keeps growing until the deadline.  `upd_card` pins
fixed rows, while `upd_iterative_card` restores adaptive scanning; an
iterative row must be a consecutive prefix `[1, ..., n]`.

The default iterator row is `[0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28]`.  A mutual
fixpoint group shares one row: members are considered in cases-theorem
order, and the first explicit row wins.  Requesting multiple models may
force an incremental SAT solver.  Merging type variables reduces scope
products but can lose models that require different cardinalities for
the original variables.

### Extending Refute

All registrations below are session-local ML state.  They do not define
HOL constants or types, and a later registration for the same key
replaces the old one.

* `register_generator ty {enumerate, random}` installs a QC generator.
  `enumerate` has type `int -> term list`; `random` has type
  `int -> rng -> term * rng`.  At least one callback must be present.
  `abstract_generator {ty, constructors, pred}` instead describes an
  abstract type through existing constructor terms and an optional
  predicate.
* `register_substrate {name, priority, accepts, preflight, compile}`
  extends or replaces the executable substrate ladder.  A substrate must compile the common plan
  and preserve candidate order and cleanup; returning `Inapplicable`
  allows `Auto` to continue.
* `register_backend backend` adds or replaces a weighted backend.
  `register_backend_with_ceiling backend ceiling` additionally declares
  a configuration- and instance-sensitive certainty ceiling.  The
  callback must overestimate: an underestimate can stop a race before a
  stronger result and violates the contract.
* `register_codatatype {tyop, case_const, constructors, witness}` validates
  the shape of `tyop`, `case_const` and `constructors` (non-empty, distinct
  constructors, each declaring - at its own declared type, not merely at
  whatever instance the caller applied it - a `tyop`-headed result type; a
  case constant with a unique valid scrutinee position, in any argument;
  the type applied to distinct type variables) and registers.  When `tyop`
  is a type the datatype database already knows as a free datatype, every
  registered constructor must also be one of that type's database
  constructors, by key, rejecting an impostor constant declared at the
  right type without needing a witness; for a type the database does not
  know, this imposes nothing.  Beyond that, shape validation cannot tell
  codata from an ordinary inductive datatype - constructor and case types
  look the same either way - so `witness = NONE` accepts the registration
  on trust, exactly as Isabelle's own `register_codatatype` does: a wrong
  registration removes the type's acyclicity constraints and can produce a
  wrong model.  The built-in registrations below are trusted this same
  way too, but through a different path: their lazy harvest calls the
  shape check directly rather than `register_codatatype`, so no witness
  ever applies to them.  `witness = SOME thm` narrows what a caller can
  get away with further: after stripping `thm`'s outer `!`s, its
  conclusion must be `?x. body`, `body` an equation with one side `aconv`
  to `x` (either orientation) and the other a constructor application
  `C a1 ... an`, where `C`'s key matches a registered constructor and `x`
  is `aconv` to some argument reached by walking `C`'s constructor spine -
  descending into an argument only when its own head is again a
  registered constructor - not merely free somewhere in it.  Every
  registered constructor's *declared* result type is `tyop`-headed, so
  `C`'s key match makes it literally that declared constant; `C` need not
  be fully applied, so `x`'s own type need not be `tyop`-headed.
  Mutual-cycle witnesses (`?x y. x = C ... y ... /\ y = C ... x ...`) and
  HOL4's per-type bisimulation theorems (`llist_bisimulation` and
  friends) are not of this shape and are not accepted; a caller holding
  one passes `witness = NONE`.  Together, the witness rules out generic
  shape defeats and the database cross-check rules out impostor
  constants for types the database knows; beyond that, the constructor
  list remains the caller's assertion.  Built-in lazy registrations
  cover `llist`, `ltree`, `itree`, `itreeTau`, `lbtree`, and `path` when
  their theories are present.
* `register_typedef {ty, abs, rep, absrep_thms}` registers existing Abs and
  Rep constants.  `absrep_thms` is either the exported bijection conjunction
  as a singleton list, or its two conjuncts `!a. abs (rep a) = a` and
  `!r. P r = (rep (abs r) = r)` as separate theorems in either order; both
  forms register identically.
  `register_quotient {qty, rty, abs, rep, equiv_thm}` registers an
  existing quotient.  `equiv_thm` must be hypothesis-free and either a
  `QUOTIENT equiv abs rep` theorem mentioning the registered `abs` and
  `rep`, or a total equivalence `!x y. R x y <=> (R x = R y)`.  Its shape
  selects the encoding: a `QUOTIENT` theorem gets the sound partial
  encoding, whereas a total equivalence drops its then redundant domain
  axiom.
* `register_frac_type {tyop, ersatz}` supplies a fraction representation
  and replacements.  `register_frac_type_rat ()` idempotently restores
  the built-in rational registration, installed by default.
  `register_frac_type_real ()` idempotently installs the analogous `real`
  registration on the same Frac carrier; unlike the rational one, it is
  opt-in and must be called explicitly, once per session, before a `real`
  goal reaches the Frac encoding.  `register_ersatz` adds an
  individual original-to-replacement constant mapping.
* `register_term_postprocessor pattern f` installs a bottom-up,
  display-only model transformation.  Matching transformations compose
  from general to specific; failures or type changes leave the raw term
  unchanged.  Certification always uses raw terms.
  `lookup_term_postprocessor ty` returns the composed callback.

The theorem sets used by MF preprocessing can be extended in a theory
script with `export_refute_simp`, `export_refute_psimp`, and
`export_refute_unfold`.  Their arguments are names of existing theorems
to export into the corresponding set.

Structural registrations require distinct type variables as parameters
and existing constants for constructors, case operators, Abs, and Rep.
The model finder also attempts a lazy, validated database harvest when it
first encounters an unregistered typedef or quotient; explicit
registration remains useful when automatic harvesting cannot recover an
otherwise standard `QUOTIENT` bridge.  Typedef harvesting accepts either
the exported bijection conjunction or its two conjuncts saved separately,
provided both live in the same theory; halves split across two theories
are not paired.  `harvest_registrations ()` is an
opt-in alternative that sweeps every theory in the session's ancestry
once, oldest first, running that same lazy harvest against every type
operator those theories declare instead of waiting for a goal to demand
it; it returns the typedef and quotient types it newly registered, so an
immediate repeat call reports nothing further.  The default remains the
demand-driven lazy harvest.

### Installing Kodkodi

The three native QC backends need no external solver.  The `kodkod`
backend is Unix-only and requires Java, the standard Unix `setsid` utility,
and Kodkodi 1.5.7.  `setsid` isolates the launched process group so Refute
can reliably terminate it.  Obtain
`kodkodi-1.5.7.tar.gz` from the Isabelle components mirror at
<https://isabelle.in.tum.de/components/>, unpack it, and set
`HOL4_KODKODI` to the component directory.  Its `jar` directory must
contain:

* `antlr-runtime-3.1.1.jar`;
* `kodkod-1.5.jar`;
* `kodkodi-1.5.7.jar`; and
* `sat4j-2.3.jar`.

Java is selected from `HOL4_JAVA_EXECUTABLE`, then `JAVA_HOME/bin/java`,
then `java` on `PATH`.  As an escape hatch,
`HOL4_KODKODI_EXEC` may name a complete standalone launcher command; it
takes precedence and is passed to the shell verbatim.  The component's
usual launcher depends on Isabelle and is not a standalone replacement.
Configuration probes are lazy and never start Java merely by loading
Refute.

SAT4J is the portable default.  JNI MiniSat, CryptoMiniSat, and Lingeling
are advertised only when their respective shared libraries are found in
the component's platform `jni` directory.  External solver environment
variables understood by Kodkodi, such as `MINISAT_HOME`,
`CRYPTOMINISAT_HOME`, `ZCHAFF_HOME`, and `RISS3G_HOME`, are retained.

### Limitations and interpretation

Counterexample search is bounded and incomplete.  In particular:

* random exhaustion, smart-generator fuel exhaustion, and narrowing at a
  finite depth normally produce `Unknown`, not a proof of validity;
* bounded-quantifier rewriting does not support offset intervals such as
  `lo <= n /\ n < hi`;
* ordinary QC requires executable terms and generators.  Unrestricted
  quantifiers over infinite types, choice, unsupported constants, and
  recursive datatypes beneath function types may make a substrate
  inapplicable;
* smart generators support positive first-order modes.  Higher-order
  relation parameters, negative modes, and general function flattening
  fall back to guards rather than becoming errors;
* subtype lifting and general function inversion are not implemented;
* narrowing finitizes function inputs.  The constant-function fallback
  for a higher-order domain is an approximation, and every depth bound
  remains incomplete;
* the model finder depends on finite scopes and on the soundness
  conditions reported in `certainty`.  Unsupported encodings are refused
  or downgraded rather than silently called genuine;
* fraction support covers `rat` by default and `real` opt-in
  (`register_frac_type_real`); certification of a Frac counterexample is
  attempted as usual and, measured, comes back inconclusive
  (`cert = NONE`), which the trust model accepts rather than blocks;
* a hand-written definition headed by `fixedPoint$lfp` or `fixedPoint$gfp`
  but absent from the `Hol_reln`/`Hol_coreln` registries is recognized if
  its monotonicity can be discharged automatically; a non-monotone
  functional falls back to plain `lfp_def`/`gfp_def` unfolding, which
  stays sound but is not refused; mutual hand-rolled fixpoints are
  recognized only as their single tupled predicate, not unrolled jointly;
* the model finder encodes machine words of concrete width only, and
  refuses width-changing and bit-permuting word operations by name;
* if lazy metadata harvest cannot validate an Abs/Rep type, MF returns
  `Unknown` and asks for an explicit typedef registration;
* `relation$WF` is encoded through a builtin ersatz definition that is
  exact on a genuinely finite or polymorphic domain, but only `Potential`
  over an intrinsically infinite domain, where the encoding's finiteness
  disjunct can never be decided from a bounded scope;
* `pred_set$CARD` and `pred_set$SUM_IMAGE` are also encoded through
  builtin ersatz definitions, both built on the same
  guarded-Hilbert-choice witness list described below
  (`pred_set$SUM_SET`, being `SUM_IMAGE I`, reaches the latter through
  its own defining equation).  These rows are liberal, not exact: a
  missing witness list degrades a verdict instead of producing a
  spurious `Genuine` refutation, but also means `NoCounterexample` is
  never available through them, regardless of the element type's
  finiteness;
* `relation$WFREC` is encoded through a pair of builtin ersatz
  definitions that tie a literal recursive equation instead of
  unfolding HOL4's own Hilbert-choice fixpoint witness function.  The
  recursion is guarded by the well-foundedness premise `WF R`, and
  matches HOL's own characterization exactly wherever that premise is
  decided; off it, the value is `unknown`, honestly reflecting that
  nothing characterizes HOL's own witness there either.  That
  `unknown` sits at a value position, not a formula position, so any
  goal that reaches this encoding loses `NoCounterexample` outright,
  not merely `Potential` as with `WF` above: an unrepresented value is
  exactly as restrictive on the liberal side of a search as on the
  sound side, so an exhausted search can no longer be trusted to mean
  no counterexample exists;
* Hilbert choice (`min$@`) is otherwise an uninterpreted function; a
  predicate with no in-scope witness would read an arbitrary value off it,
  so the model finder inserts a guard at every syntactic occurrence and
  reports the choice's value as unrepresented instead when no witness is
  in scope.  A later simplification can erase an inserted guard's `if`,
  but the encoding stays sound in that case too, and a fired guard's
  bounds signal (below) survives the erasure regardless.  Two escapes
  stand the guard down where the unguarded read is independently sound: a
  predicate provably empty in HOL (decided by a fixed, terminating
  decision procedure under a syntactic fragment and size gate, never a
  search), and a predicate over `bool`, always exactly represented.  A
  fired guard also blocks `NoCounterexample`: a search that finds no model
  reports a bounds-relative `Unknown` instead, since a truncated witness
  cannot be told apart from a genuinely empty one; and
* HolRefute is built only with Poly/ML.  Kodkodi adds the Unix, Java, and
  local-component restrictions described above.

A counterexample is useful evidence that a statement is false, especially
when `cert = SOME thm`.  `NoCounterexample` is the stronger diagnostic claim
that the whole search space was covered, but it is not a HOL proof object.
`Unknown` is only guidance for changing bounds, generators, backends, or the
conjecture.

\index{HolRefute|)}
